{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"8af4c38a-2044-451e-9e5f-8b28b7f626b4","title":"Completing the square with an area model"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8af4c38a-2044-451e-9e5f-8b28b7f626b4","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with x squared plus six x. For positive x, a square of side x represents the x squared term.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Completing the square"},{"op":"equation","id":"start","region":"A2","latex":"x^2+6x"},{"op":"figure","id":"model","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-1,8],"y":[-1,8.2],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"base","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[4,4],[0,4]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[2,2],"text":"x²"},{"kind":"measure","from":[0,-0.4],"to":[4,-0.4],"label":"x"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-0.4,0],"to":[-0.4,4],"label":"x"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8af4c38a-2044-451e-9e5f-8b28b7f626b4","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Split six x into two equal rectangles, each with area three x. Place one above the square and one beside it.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"strips","on":"model","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"topstrip","points":[[0,4],[4,4],[4,7],[0,7]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"polyline","id":"rightstrip","points":[[4,0],[7,0],[7,4],[4,4]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[2,5.5],"text":"3x"},{"kind":"text","at":[5.5,2],"text":"3x"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-0.4,4],"to":[-0.4,7],"label":"3"},{"kind":"measure","from":[4,-0.4],"to":[7,-0.4],"label":"3"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"split","anchor":{"el":"start","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2+6x=x^2+3x+3x"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8af4c38a-2044-451e-9e5f-8b28b7f626b4","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The missing corner is three by three. Adding nine fills that corner and makes a complete square.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"corner_add","on":"model","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"corner","points":[[4,4],[7,4],[7,7],[4,7]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[5.5,5.5],"text":"9"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"added","anchor":{"el":"split","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2=3^2=9"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why do we add nine rather than six to complete this square?","expected_gist":"The missing corner has side 3, so its area is 9."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8af4c38a-2044-451e-9e5f-8b28b7f626b4","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Each full side is x plus three. The completed square includes nine extra, so subtract nine to recover the original expression.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"whole_side","on":"model","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[0,7.6],"to":[7,7.6],"label":"x + 3"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"square_eq","anchor":{"el":"added","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2+6x+9=(x+3)^2"},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"square_eq","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{x^2+6x=(x+3)^2-9}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
